analysisWe need to produce an assert statement matching the comment.

We have a method add(Attribute attribute) that calls super.add(attribute); then comment: "If the attribute is null, then the last element in the list is also null; if the attribute is not null, then it equals the last element in the list."

We need to express: after the method, the last element (i.e., element at index size()-1) is null iff attribute is null, else equals attribute.

We can use this.get(this.size()-1) to get last element (type Attribute). Since method returns void, we don't need \result.

We need to refer to attribute parameter. Use attribute variable.

Spec: (attribute == null) => this.get(this.size()-1) == null && (attribute != null) => this.get(this.size()-1).equals(attribute). However need to be careful with null equality: we can use == for null.

Thus assert: (attribute == null) => this.get(this.size()-1) == null && (attribute != null) => this.get(this.size()-1).equals(attribute);

But parentheses needed: (attribute == null) => (this.get(this.size()-1) == null) && (attribute != null) => (this.get(this.size()-1).equals(attribute));

But note precedence: && lower than =>? Actually => has lower priority than &&, so need parentheses around each implication if combined with &&. So we can write:

assert (attribute == null) => (this.get(this.size()-1) == null) && (attribute != null) => (this.get(this.size()-1).equals(attribute));

That seems correct.

Check that we can call this.get(this.size()-1) after addition; size() returns int, get returns Attribute.

Edge case: if list was empty before, size after addition is old size +1, last index is size()-1.

Thus final answer.

